To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries in row 4 other than $a_{43}=2$ equal to $0.$

To do this, we can apply the following elementary column operations:

$$
\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 5 & -9 & -8 \\ -4 & -7 & 3 & 1 \\ 0 & -9 & 9 & 2 \\ 6 & 10 & 2 & 10\end{bmatrix}
\xrightarrow{C_{1}:=C_{1}-3C_{3}}\begin{bmatrix}26 & 5 & -9 & -8 \\ -13 & -7 & 3 & 1 \\ -27 & -9 & 9 & 2 \\ 0 & 10 & 2 & 10\end{bmatrix}
\xrightarrow{C_{2}:=C_{2}-5C_{3}}\begin{bmatrix}26 & 50 & -9 & -8 \\ -13 & -22 & 3 & 1 \\ -27 & -54 & 9 & 2 \\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 10\end{bmatrix}
\xrightarrow{C_{4}:=C_{4}-5C_{3}}\begin{bmatrix}26 & 50 & -9 & 37 \\ -13 & -22 & 3 & -14 \\ -27 & -54 & 9 & -43 \\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0\end{bmatrix}=B
$$

Therefore, the corresponding elementary column operations are

$C_{1}:=C_{1}-3C_{3}$ and $C_{4}:=C_{4}-5C_{3}$.

To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries in row 2 other than $a_{21}=2$ equal to $0.$

To do this, we can apply the following elementary column operations:

$$
\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 & 1 & 7 \\ 2 & 6 & -10 & 2 \\ 4 & 4 & 6 & 1 \\ 6 & 2 & -9 & 5\end{bmatrix}
\xrightarrow{C_{2}:=C_{2}-3C_{1}}\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 & 7 \\ 2 & 0 & -10 & 2 \\ 4 & -8 & 6 & 1 \\ 6 & -16 & -9 & 5\end{bmatrix}
\xrightarrow{C_{3}:=C_{3}+5C_{1}}\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & -4 & 7 \\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\ 4 & -8 & 26 & 1 \\ 6 & -16 & 21 & 5\end{bmatrix}
